/**
 * run_cc_debug — the DEBUG-profile sibling of `run_cc` (the `teko run` build path):
 * compile the generated C `cfile` into `binary` with `build_cc_argv(..., true)`, so
 * the link line carries `-g` (DWARF for the E4 native stack trace, resolved against
 * the `.tsym` map) on top of the already-unoptimized, assertions-on release line.
 * The ONLY difference from `run_cc` is the debug flag, keeping the two paths aligned.
 *
 * @param cfile   the generated C translation unit
 * @param binary  the output native executable path
 * @param m       the resolved manifest
 * @param prog    the checked program (extern-reachability)
 * @return        the `cc` process exit code (0 on success)
 */
fn run_cc_debug(cfile: str, binary: str, m: Manifest, prog: checker::TProgram) -> i32 {
    invoke_cc(build_cc_argv(cfile, binary, m, prog, true, 0), binary)
}

/**
 * run_cc_for_level — the D1.6 CLI-surface sibling of `run_cc`: like it, but adds `-g` to the cc
 * link line when `debug` requests it (`DebugInfo::Lines`), so `teko build --debug=lines` reaches
 * a real effect on the C-backend link path via the SAME `build_cc_argv(..., debug: bool, opt)`
 * mechanism `run_cc_debug` already proves for `teko run`.
 *
 * Deliberately does NOT touch `run_cc_debug`/`build_debug_binary` (the `teko run` build path):
 * per the design doc's §7.5 rule 3, `teko run` keeps its own always-on debug profile and does not
 * start requiring this flag. The two axes stay independent.
 *
 * On the own-native backend (the default since 2026-07-26) `debug` reaches a real effect too, via
 * a SEPARATE path: `backend` never reaches THIS function on that route (`emit_native` returns
 * first) — the ELF/Mach-O sink is `emit_native`'s own `build_dwarf_sink_for` (D1.4/D1.5), not
 * `build_cc_argv`'s `-g`.
 *
